    category: "Testing Advanced React",
    expectedAnswer: "Context propagates values through a component subtree. The key production concern is provider value identity: changing the value can invalidate consumers, so split contexts and stabilize values where measurement shows benefit.",
    deepExplanation: "Example:\n\n```ts\nimport { createContext, useContext, useMemo, useState } from \"react\";\n\ntype Session = { userId: string } | null;\nconst SessionContext = createContext<Session>(null);\n\nexport function SessionProvider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {\n  const [session] = useState<Session>({ userId: \"u-1\" });\n  const value = useMemo(() => session, [session]);\n\n  return (\n    <SessionContext.Provider value={value}>\n      {children}\n    </SessionContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n\nexport function useSession() {\n  return useContext(SessionContext);\n}\n```\n\nWhy it matters: It affects correctness, responsiveness, maintainability or scalability. Senior interviews expect trade-off analysis, not API memorization. Enterprise systems require observability, testing, accessibility and safe migration paths.",
